<ProjectLayout
  title="Engineering Rocket"
  number="05"
  accentColor="#fb923c"
  heroColor="#180a06"
  heroImage="/projects/engineering-rocket/cover.webp"
  heroImageWidth={1200}
  heroImageHeight={1600}
  intro="A student rocket club in Kazakhstan, run out of a faculty of science and technology. We designed airframes in CAD, printed them section by section, built the avionics on microcontrollers and took the result out to a field. I led the club."
  problem="A student club has no wind tunnel, no machine shop and no budget for a machined airframe. What it has is a 3D printer, a bench, and people who have never built a rocket before. So the real problem was never aerodynamics — it was making the whole loop cheap and repeatable enough that a beginner could design a part on Monday, hold it on Wednesday and understand why it failed on Friday. Every design decision that follows was made against that constraint."
  approach="We modeled each section separately — nose cone, body tube, fin can, avionics bay — so a failure meant reprinting one part rather than the whole rocket, and so several people could work in parallel. Everything went through the slicer before it went on the printer: wall count, infill and print orientation were part of the design, because on a printed airframe those decide whether a fin shears off. The avionics were built on microcontroller boards, wired and tested on the bench before they ever went into a body. Assembly and finishing were done by hand."
  solution="What the club produced was a working design loop rather than a single rocket: CAD models that print without supports where it matters, an avionics bay sized around the boards we actually had, and printed sections that fit together and survive handling. New members could join at any point in the loop, which is what kept the club running rather than collapsing into one person's project."
  outcome="The work was presented at the faculty, and the club was recognised with certificates of thanks. The honest limits of this page: the flight record, the altitudes reached and the exact division of design work between members are not documented in the material I have, so they are deliberately absent rather than estimated. The engineering that is visible — the printed sections, the sliced models, the avionics on the bench, the field — is what this page claims."
  gallery={gallery}
/>
